From: Hans Christian Winther-Sorensen <hc@wsh.no>
Subject: [PATCH] sparc64: name the root node SUNW,Ultra-5_10, as OBP does on
 the machine QEMU's sun4u models

The root node was "OpenBiosTeam,OpenBIOS". Solaris installs the
platform-specific packages by the root node's name: the Solaris 2.6
installer adds the UltraSPARC-IIi links (the cpu module for this CPU)
only when that name is a platform it knows, such as SUNW,Ultra-5_10.
Without them the installed system stopped right after the boot block
with "krtld: error during initial load/link phase". QEMU's sun4u is an
Ultra 5/10 in all that matters here (sabre, simba, the CMD646 IDE, the
ebus), and the Solaris 2.5.1 and 2.6 CDs for it are Ultra 5/10 media.

Name the root node as OBP names an Ultra 5/10. Solaris looks for its
kernel under /platform/<name> first and falls back to /platform/sun4u,
so systems installed under the old name keep booting.
---
 arch/sparc64/tree.fs | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/sparc64/tree.fs b/arch/sparc64/tree.fs
index 814de6c..aac21fc 100644
--- a/arch/sparc64/tree.fs
+++ b/arch/sparc64/tree.fs
@@ -98,6 +98,12 @@ finish-device
 " /options" find-device
   " disk" encode-string " boot-from" property
 
+" /" find-device
+  \ The name OBP gives the root of an Ultra 5/10, the machine QEMU's sun4u is modelled on: Solaris
+  \ installs its platform packages by this name (2.6's installer adds the UltraSPARC-IIi links
+  \ only for a platform it knows), and boots from /platform/<name> with sun4u as the fallback
+  " SUNW,Ultra-5_10" encode-string " name" property
+
 " /openprom" find-device
   " OBP 3.10.24 1999/01/01 01:01" encode-string " version" property
   \ OBP has it: the Solaris 2.5.1 boot program loads the kernel's text and
